      "text": "To find the maximum value of \\(\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2}\\), we start by expanding the numerator:\n\n\\[\n(x + y)^2 = x^2 + 2xy + y^2.\n\\]\n\nThus, the expression becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} = \\frac{x^2 + 2xy + y^2}{x^2 + y^2}.\n\\]\n\nWe can separate this into two parts:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2 + 2xy + y^2}{x^2 + y^2} = \\frac{x^2}{x^2 + y^2} + \\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2} + \\frac{y^2}{x^2 + y^2}.\n\\]\n\nLet \\(a = \\frac{x^2}{x^2 + y^2}\\), \\(b = \\frac{y^2}{x^2 + y^2}\\), and \\(c = \\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2}\\). Then the expression simplifies to:\n\n\\[\na + b + c.\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the maximum value of \\(a + b + c\\). First, we note that \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are both in the interval \\((0, 1)\\) because \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are positive real numbers. We can use the method of Lagrange multipliers or the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality to find the maximum value of \\(a + b + c\\).\n\nBy the AM-GM inequality, we have:\n\n\\[\na + b \\geq 2\\sqrt{ab}.\n\\]\n\nAlso, by the AM-GM inequality again, we have:\n\n\\[\na + b \\leq 1.\n\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\n\\[\n0 < a + b \\leq 1.\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the bounds for \\(c\\). Notice that:\n\n\\[\nc = \\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2} = \\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2}.\n\\]\n\nBy the identity \\((x + y)^2 = x^2 + 2xy + y^2\\), we have:\n\n\\[\nc = \\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2} = \\frac{2xy}{(x + y)^2 - 2xy}.\n\\]\n\nTo find a tighter bound for \\(c\\), we use the fact that:\n\n\\[\nc \\leq \\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2}.\n\\]\n\nBy the AM-GM inequality, we have:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + y^2 \\geq 2xy.\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2} \\leq 1.\n\\]\n\nBut we need a more precise bound. By the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality in the form \\((x^2 + y^2)(1^2 + 1^2) \\geq (x + y)^2\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n2(x^2 + y^2) \\geq (x + y)^2,\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n1 \\geq \\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2}.\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\nc \\leq 1.\n\\]\n\nCombining these results, we get:\n\n\\[\n0 < c \\leq 1.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of \\(a + b + c\\) is 1, which is achieved when \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = 0\\), and \\(c = 0\\). This occurs when \\(x = y\\). Substituting \\(x = y\\) into the expression, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(x + x)^2}{x^2 + x^2} = \\frac{(2x)^2}{2x^2} = \\frac{4x^2}{2x^2} = 2.\n\\]\n\nThus, the maximum value is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}.\n\\]",
